all right one other rule that's going to  reportedly be tweaked and this is  another report from judy batista of nfl  media and look  i walk a fine line anytime i point this  out any reporter who works for nfl media  is working for the nfl there's an  inherent conflict of interest there and  a pattern develops every time they're  going to have one of these meetings  somebody from the competition committee  or the league office tells judy batista  what is expected to happen and it feels  official because it's coming from nfl  media now  i don't know who the person is that's  supplying the information is it somebody  who's trying to tip the scale one way or  the other is it somebody who has an  opinion that is emanating from the  existence of the report is it a trial  balloon that's being put out there by  someone to see how people react to it we  never know that but the bottom line is  it feels official but we shouldn't  regard it as official it's not official  until they do it and this potential  change relates to the replay review  expansion to encompass past interference  there's a chance that the rules will be  revised to  make pass interference reviewable only  through the coaches challenge not  through the automatic system where  there's replay review available without  a coach's challenge final two minutes of  either half over time after a touchdown  or after a turnover  um  the idea is as explained in the various  tweets that were posted by judy batista  that there would be  greater consistency in the standard if  the league office isn't involved in the  automatic review it look it makes no  sense  because the coaches aren't reviewing the  play the coaches are just initiating the  process  and for the automatic replay review it  doesn't take much to initiate the  process  see i  i i think what they're trying to do is  reduce the number of times that you  would feel compelled to initiate the  process because the way it's supposed to  work to overturn the ruling on the field  through the replay review process  there should be clear and obvious  evidence that the ruling on the field  was incorrect to initiate the process  the way it's always been  it has to be clear and obvious that the  ruling on the field was correct if it's  not clear and obvious that it was  correct then you fall into that gulf  where you try to figure out whether or  not there's clear and obvious evidence  that was incorrect see here's what  becomes a problem with past interference  it's going to be very easy to hit the  button and say we need to give this a  second look because  there'll be plenty of cases of hand  fighting and jostling and incidental  contact where it's not going to be clear  and obvious that there wasn't pass  interference and so if that's the case  you don't want to have a full-blown  replay review every time there's a score  every time there's a pass thrown in the  final two minutes every time there's a  turnover  and then you take the time you do it you  say no that it wasn't enough to overturn  it that's the problem so  the standard  threshold  for triggering replay review it's going  to get tripped a lot as it relates to  past interference and you know what i  haven't thought of that one either  sometimes it makes sense to sound this  stuff out even if i'm doing it somewhat  clumsily and hey does the fact that i  admit it make it any better probably not  but you could maybe sense the gears  turning in my brain here i see what  they're getting at it's not a matter of  having a consistent standard it's a  matter of not wanting to apply the  standard that applies to  when  the replay official says we need to have  a full-blown replay review so i think i  understand what they're trying to do  here  it just it it creates a potential mess  if you don't have any red flags left  because you get two unless you're right  on the first two then you get a third  um and you have to have a timeout left  or you can't use the red flag anyway  because you lose a timeout as the  punishment for being wrong they maybe  need to relook  at the challenge system if they're gonna  do this because  there's a chance that you could have an  egregious call like the one we saw in  ram saints in the nfc championship game  and you don't have a red flag or you  don't have a timeout at your disposal to  correct what would be a horrible call  that needs to be rectified immediately  so look this is not i think the problem  is  the competition committee tried to drag  its feet from january into march and not  propose any meaningful change and they  got to the league meeting they realized  oh crap  they really want this to change and they  threw something together quickly and  they really didn't think it all through  and frankly i hadn't thought it through  from the perspective of how easy it is  and how little it will take to trigger a  full-blown replay review  of potential pass interference  hi i'm mike tarico and thanks for  watching make sure to hit subscribe for  the latest news and highlights from nbc  sports
 

